WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.200
Hey, I just want to pause and say you're doing an amazing job.

00:03.640 --> 00:05.720
Keep that energy high and stay focused.

00:06.080 --> 00:07.220
And guess what?

00:07.220 --> 00:10.320
You are officially more than halfway done with the pre-training.

00:10.560 --> 00:12.460
That is a huge milestone.

00:12.460 --> 00:15.520
Here at RoofER, we believe in celebrating every step of your journey.

00:15.900 --> 00:17.820
So take a breath. You've got this.

00:18.200 --> 00:19.560
All right. Section one.

00:19.560 --> 00:20.640
Welcome to the RoofDocs.

00:20.640 --> 00:22.820
We are genuinely pumped to have you on the team.

00:23.120 --> 00:27.160
Our mission is to give you all the tools you need to become our next big success story.

00:27.760 --> 00:30.360
The opportunity you have in front of you is just massive.

00:30.740 --> 00:32.500
And it all starts with understanding your role.

00:32.500 --> 00:35.440
So what's the goal when you're at a homeowner's door?

00:35.540 --> 00:37.040
It's actually really simple.

00:37.040 --> 00:39.800
You're there to offer a completely free inspection to see if they have

00:39.800 --> 00:41.340
qualifyable storm damage.

00:41.340 --> 00:42.840
Think of yourself as a detective.

00:42.840 --> 00:46.240
You know, you're looking for clues that are going to solve a huge problem

00:46.240 --> 00:47.440
for that homeowner.

00:47.440 --> 00:49.080
Let's move on to section two.

00:49.080 --> 00:50.620
Roofing 101.

00:50.620 --> 00:53.720
To be a RoofDoc, you've got to know the anatomy of your patient, right?

00:54.360 --> 00:58.220
Understanding the product you're dealing with is the absolute first step

00:58.560 --> 01:00.560
to being able to spot the problems.

01:00.560 --> 01:03.360
Out in the field, you're going to see a few different types of roofs.

01:03.820 --> 01:07.120
By far, the most common is going to be asphalt shingles.

01:07.420 --> 01:11.760
But you'll also run into metal roofs, those beautiful cedar shake roofs

01:12.100 --> 01:14.060
or their synthetic lookalikes.

01:14.060 --> 01:18.880
And for flat or low slope roofs, you'll see stuff like TPO or rolled asphalt.

01:19.380 --> 01:21.580
It's key to get familiar with what you're looking at.

01:21.940 --> 01:25.540
You'll also notice that most houses have one of two main roof shapes.

01:25.940 --> 01:27.240
You've got the gable roof.

01:27.240 --> 01:29.320
That's the classic triangle A-frame shape.

01:29.680 --> 01:33.080
And then you've got the hipid roof, which has slopes on all four sides

01:33.080 --> 01:34.580
that come up to a point.

01:34.580 --> 01:37.720
Trust me, recognizing these will become second nature in no time.

01:38.080 --> 01:40.960
Now, let's talk about one of the most critical parts of any roof,

01:40.960 --> 01:43.060
but it's one that a lot of people overlook.

01:44.000 --> 01:45.960
The flashing.

01:45.960 --> 01:49.000
This thin piece of metal is basically the unsung hero

01:49.020 --> 01:52.460
that keeps water out of the most vulnerable spots.

01:53.260 --> 01:56.400
Think chimneys, skylights, where the roof meets a wall.

01:56.760 --> 01:59.640
When the flashing fails, leaks are pretty much a guarantee,

01:59.740 --> 02:02.240
which is why it's so important for you to inspect it carefully.

02:02.600 --> 02:04.480
OK, section three.

02:04.480 --> 02:05.780
This is it.

02:05.780 --> 02:07.640
This is the heart of what you do.

02:07.640 --> 02:08.840
Decoding storm damage.

02:08.840 --> 02:11.720
This is where you take everything you're learning and put it to the test,

02:12.040 --> 02:14.140
learning how to find your targets.

02:14.140 --> 02:16.120
This is what it's all about.

02:16.120 --> 02:18.260
You're basically looking for two main culprits.

02:18.520 --> 02:20.080
Hail and wind.

02:20.080 --> 02:23.580
The easiest way to think about it is this hail is a bruiser.

02:24.020 --> 02:26.420
It pounds the shingle and leaves these circular dents,

02:26.660 --> 02:29.120
knocking off the protective granules.

02:29.120 --> 02:31.400
Wind, on the other hand, is a lifter.

02:31.400 --> 02:34.700
It gets up underneath the shingle and creases it, lifts it up,

02:34.700 --> 02:38.260
or sometimes just rips it clean off to keep all your photo reports

02:38.260 --> 02:39.400
super clear and simple.

02:39.400 --> 02:41.900
We use a really easy system.

02:41.900 --> 02:43.900
You're going to have a piece of chalk with you on the roof.

02:44.600 --> 02:46.660
Any time you see hail damage, you circle it.

02:47.280 --> 02:49.920
Any time you see wind damage, you put a slash through it.

02:50.520 --> 02:53.680
It's this simple visual language that everyone from our team

02:53.820 --> 02:56.220
to the insurance adjusters will get in a heartbeat.

02:56.220 --> 02:58.680
So what are the dead giveaways for hail damage?

02:59.060 --> 03:01.520
Well, first you'll see those telltale circular bruises

03:01.520 --> 03:02.920
on the shingles themselves.

03:02.920 --> 03:05.360
Then you need to look in the gutters.

03:05.360 --> 03:08.060
If you see a pile of granules, that's a huge red flag.

03:08.900 --> 03:11.400
See, those granules are like sunscreen for the shingle.

03:12.120 --> 03:15.460
Without them, the asphalt underneath just gets destroyed by the sun

03:15.460 --> 03:17.360
and starts to break down fast.

03:17.360 --> 03:19.420
And finally, always, always check the soft metals.

03:19.880 --> 03:21.880
Dents on gutters, downspouts and vents.

03:21.880 --> 03:24.760
That's undeniable proof that a serious hailstorm came through.

03:25.080 --> 03:27.180
Section four, the inspection playbook.

03:28.380 --> 03:29.880
OK, so now you know what to look for.

03:29.880 --> 03:32.580
Let's put it all together into a simple, repeatable process

03:32.580 --> 03:35.420
that you're going to use on every single house, every single time.

03:35.620 --> 03:37.460
No guesswork. This is it.

03:37.720 --> 03:40.060
This is your playbook. Follow it every time.

03:40.420 --> 03:43.800
You start on the ground first, snap a photo of the mailbox or house number.

03:44.120 --> 03:46.340
It just identifies the property.

03:46.340 --> 03:48.540
Second, take a wide shot of the whole house.

03:48.840 --> 03:50.960
Next, you do a full lap around the house,

03:50.960 --> 03:54.040
taking pictures of any collateral damage on all four sides.

03:54.340 --> 03:59.180
We build our case from the ground up, then and only then do you get on the roof.

03:59.440 --> 04:02.800
Once you're up there, you'll get photos of damage to the roof vents and metals.

04:03.020 --> 04:06.440
And finally, you'll mark and photograph all the shingle damage.

04:07.020 --> 04:10.860
Once you get the hang of it, you'll fly through this in about 15 or 20 minutes.

04:11.220 --> 04:14.820
Now, before we go any further, let's talk about the absolute number one priority

04:14.820 --> 04:17.320
on any job, your safety.

04:17.320 --> 04:19.560
This is completely non-negotiable.

04:19.560 --> 04:21.900
If a roof is too steep, if it's wet,

04:22.260 --> 04:26.360
if you just have a bad feeling about it for any reason, you do not do the inspection.

04:26.760 --> 04:30.300
Period. Always make sure your ladder is on solid level ground.

04:30.660 --> 04:34.000
And the very first thing you should buy, a pair of cougar paws.

04:34.400 --> 04:37.000
Those things grip the roof and they will save your hide.

04:37.340 --> 04:39.700
All right. Section five, building the case.

04:39.720 --> 04:43.460
This is where all your hard work, all your detailed inspection photos

04:43.460 --> 04:45.700
turn into a massive win for the homeowner.

04:46.300 --> 04:48.460
Your pictures aren't just pictures.

04:48.460 --> 04:51.620
They're the critical evidence that gets a family the new roof they deserve

04:51.860 --> 04:53.660
paid for by their insurance.

04:53.660 --> 04:56.620
This quote right here, this perfectly sums up your role.

04:57.060 --> 04:59.520
You're not just a salesperson, you're an advocate.

05:00.020 --> 05:02.660
You really have to think of yourself as a lawyer for the homeowner.

05:03.100 --> 05:05.160
All that damage you find, that's your evidence.

05:05.420 --> 05:08.300
Every dented gutter, every torn window screen,

05:08.580 --> 05:10.740
every single bruised shingle.

05:10.740 --> 05:13.540
It's all a part of the undeniable case you're building for them.

05:13.940 --> 05:17.440
And that is exactly why that collateral damage is so important.

05:17.740 --> 05:21.780
I mean, a few dings on a downspout might not seem like a huge deal functionally,

05:21.780 --> 05:25.140
but what they do is prove that a storm strong enough to dent metal

05:25.140 --> 05:27.480
just rolled over this property.

05:27.480 --> 05:29.440
It builds a complete story.

05:29.440 --> 05:32.300
It backs up the evidence on the shingles, and it makes it really,

05:32.300 --> 05:34.440
really hard for an insurance company to say no.

05:34.740 --> 05:36.700
All right. Last section.

05:36.700 --> 05:38.700
Let's talk about your mission.

05:38.700 --> 05:42.380
Because after all the training, after all the theory, it boils down to this.

05:43.080 --> 05:45.980
Get signups, get bonuses and close houses.

05:46.880 --> 05:50.540
This is where you turn all that knowledge into real success for yourself

05:50.540 --> 05:52.380
and for the homeowners you're helping.

05:52.380 --> 05:54.580
So what's the magic number?

05:54.880 --> 05:59.020
What's the one activity that is guaranteed to bring you success in this business?

05:59.360 --> 06:01.420
It's this one. 70.

06:01.780 --> 06:03.940
That's the minimum number of doors you need to knock on

06:03.940 --> 06:06.020
every single day you're out there working.

06:06.020 --> 06:08.120
It's all about activity and consistency.

06:08.620 --> 06:10.160
That's the whole game.

06:10.160 --> 06:11.320
So there you have it.

06:11.320 --> 06:14.420
You've got the foundation, you've got the knowledge and you've got the playbook.

06:14.920 --> 06:17.220
Now, the only thing left to do is go put it into action.

06:17.560 --> 06:20.520
Get out there, start knocking and start helping homeowners.

06:20.920 --> 06:22.520
Welcome to the RoofER team.

